jurisdiction
provenance

The question:

> Are these two structures constitutionally equivalent, or merely component-equivalent?




---

VI. 🦆 FOOL — FLATTENING ATTACK

FOOL attacks the tempting optimization:

(A ⊕ B) ⊕ C
↓
A ⊕ B ⊕ C

FOOL asks:

> What constitutional information disappeared when the tree was flattened?



Potentially:

intermediate authority
intermediate evidence
intermediate determination
intermediate jurisdiction
intermediate constraints

Therefore:

STRUCTURAL FLATTENING SHALL NOT ERASE CONSTITUTIONALLY RELEVANT INTERMEDIATE STATES.


---

VII. ⚖️ TRIAS — HIGHER-ORDER DETERMINATION

TRIAS receives:

COMPOSITION_TREE
COMPONENT_VALIDITY
INTERMEDIATE_DETERMINATIONS
ASSOCIATIVITY_STATUS
JURISDICTIONS
AUTHORITY_GRAPH
CONSEQUENCE_GRAPH
EVIDENCE
TEMPORAL_CONTEXT
FOOL_CHALLENGES

and determines:

HIGHER_ORDER_VALID
STRUCTURALLY_EQUIVALENT
CONDITIONALLY_EQUIVALENT
NON_EQUIVALENT
COMPOSITION_INVALID
HOLD

A critical rule:

TRIAS SHALL NOT DECLARE
T₁ = T₂
MERELY BECAUSE
LEAVES(T₁) = LEAVES(T₂)

Same components do not guarantee same constitutional composition.


---

VIII. 💧 WATERMARK — STRUCTURAL PROVENANCE

WATERMARK now records:

W_COMPOSITION {
    composition_id
    structure
    ordered_operands
    nested_compositions
    intermediate_states
    associativity_status
    authority
    jurisdiction
    evidence
    determination
    epoch
    provenance
}

Thus:

(A⊕B)⊕C

and:

A⊕(B⊕C)

receive distinct provenance identities unless equivalence is independently established.


---

IX. ⚓ QUAY — NESTED LINEAGE

QUAY preserves:

A
B
 ↓
X = A⊕B
 ↓
C
 ↓
Y = X⊕C

The intermediate:

X

cannot disappear simply because:

Y

is the current state.

Historical and structural lineage remain:

A → X → Y
B → X
C → Y

This allows a verifier to reconstruct:

> Where did the higher-order state actually come from?




---

X. 🔄 COMPOSITIONAL REFACTORING

R7.71 introduces:

COMPOSITIONAL_REFACTORING

Suppose:

(A⊕B)⊕C

is transformed into:

A⊕(B⊕C)

That is not automatically an implementation optimization.

It is a constitutional transformation.

The system must prove:

EQUIVALENCE(original, transformed)

under the relevant constitutional semantics.

If equivalence cannot be established:

REFACTORING = NEW COMPOSITION

and must undergo fresh validation.


---

XI. 🛑 SEMANTIC REFACTORING BOUNDARY

A particularly important distinction:

CODE REFACTORING
≠
CONSTITUTIONAL REFACTORING

A compiler may regard two structures as equivalent.

HORTUS cannot.

HORTUS asks whether:

STATE
AUTHORITY
MEANING
PROVENANCE
CONSEQUENCE

remain constitutionally equivalent.


---

XII. 🧩 INTERMEDIATE STATE AUTHORITY

An intermediate composition may create:

X = A⊕B

and X may acquire properties.

But:

AUTH(X)

must not automatically follow from:

AUTH(A)
+
AUTH(B)

Therefore:

HIGHER-ORDER COMPOSITION SHALL NOT MANUFACTURE INTERMEDIATE AUTHORITY.

The same principle recursively applies:

AUTH(X)
+
AUTH(C)
≠
AUTH(Y)

without independent basis.


---

XIII. 🌐 NESTED JURISDICTION

Consider:

A → J₁
B → J₁
C → J₂

Then:

X = A⊕B

may legitimately exist under:

J₁

while:

X⊕C

crosses into:

J₂

The transition requires explicit jurisdictional analysis.

Therefore:

COMPOSITIONAL_JURISDICTION_TRANSITION

is now detectable.


---

XIV. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — ASSOCIATIVITY TEST

BRAINPOWER may construct:

T₁ = (A⊕B)⊕C
T₂ = A⊕(B⊕C)

and evaluate:

STRUCTURE
SEMANTICS
AUTHORITY
DEPENDENCY
CONSEQUENCE
STATE

Result:

T₁ ≡ T₂

only when the equivalence is actually proven.

Otherwise:

T₁ ≠ T₂

or:

EQUIVALENCE_UNPROVEN


---

XV. 🦆 FOOL — ORDER-SENSITIVE AUTHORITY ATTACK

FOOL asks:

> What if the first composition changes the authority available to the second?



Example:

A⊕B
↓
creates X
↓
X authorizes C

versus:

B⊕C
↓
creates Z
↓
Z authorizes A

The leaf set is identical.

The constitutional authority graph is not.

Thus:

ORDER OF COMPOSITION MAY CHANGE AUTHORITY.


---

XVI. ⚠️ COMPOSITIONAL SIDE EFFECT

R7.71 introduces:

COMPOSITIONAL_SIDE_EFFECT

A composition has a side effect when constructing an intermediate state changes the constitutional environment in which later composition occurs.

Conceptually:

A⊕B
↓
X
↓
environment changes
↓
X⊕C

versus:

B⊕C
↓
Y
↓
environment changes
↓
A⊕Y

The resulting states may differ even when all components are individually identical.


