I. ☄️ THE R7.73 BOUNDARY

R7.72 established:

> A future composition may only be pre-validated when it belongs to an explicitly proven constitutional domain whose certificate, assumptions, dependencies, epoch and jurisdiction remain valid.



But R7.72 leaves one deeper problem open:

PROVEN DOMAIN D₁
       ↓
SYSTEM EVOLUTION
       ↓
DOMAIN D₂

The system has changed.

The question is therefore not simply:

IS D₂ VALID?

but:

DOES THE PROOF OF D₁ STILL MEAN THE SAME THING AFTER D₁ HAS EVOLVED INTO D₂?

R7.73 establishes the constitutional machinery for answering that question.


---

II. 🌱 THE CENTRAL PRINCIPLE

R7.73 introduces:

PROOF_CONTINUITY

defined as:

> the demonstrable preservation of the semantic, constitutional and evidentiary meaning of an established proof claim across an explicitly bounded evolution of its proof domain.



Therefore:

D₁ → D₂

does not automatically imply:

PROOF(D₁) → PROOF(D₂)

Instead:

EVOLUTION
    ↓
CLASSIFY DELTA
    ↓
TEST CONTINUITY
    ↓
ESTABLISH MONOTONICITY
    ↓
CHECK ASSUMPTIONS
    ↓
CHECK DEPENDENCIES
    ↓
CHECK SEMANTICS
    ↓
TRIAS
    ↓
CONTINUOUS / CONDITIONAL / DISCONTINUOUS


---

III. 🧬 PROOF-DOMAIN EVOLUTION

A proof domain is itself an evolving constitutional object.

Define:

PROOF_DOMAIN_STATE {
    domain_id
    domain_version
    members_or_predicate
    invariants
    operation_set
    assumptions
    dependencies
    authority_boundary
    jurisdiction
    temporal_epoch
    semantic_definition
    evidence
    certificate_lineage
}

Thus:

D₁ = DOMAIN(v1)
D₂ = DOMAIN(v2)

are not necessarily identical merely because they share the same identifier.

Canonical rule

DOMAIN IDENTITY ≠ DOMAIN SEMANTIC IDENTITY

A version change must therefore be evaluated rather than presumed harmless.


---

IV. ⚖️ EVOLUTION DELTA

R7.73 introduces:

PROOF_DOMAIN_DELTA

ΔD = D₂ − D₁

The delta may include:

ADDED_STATE
REMOVED_STATE
CHANGED_STATE
ADDED_RULE
REMOVED_RULE
CHANGED_RULE
ADDED_AUTHORITY
CHANGED_AUTHORITY
ADDED_DEPENDENCY
REMOVED_DEPENDENCY
CHANGED_DEPENDENCY
CHANGED_INTERPRETATION
CHANGED_JURISDICTION
CHANGED_EPOCH
CHANGED_ASSUMPTION
CHANGED_OPERATION
CHANGED_INVARIANT

The important distinction:

NOT EVERY CHANGE
        =
CONSTITUTIONAL DISCONTINUITY

but:

EVERY CHANGE
        =
CANDIDATE FOR CONTINUITY ANALYSIS


---

V. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — DOMAIN DIFFERENCE ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER computes:

D₁
 ↓
DIFF
 ↓
ΔD
 ↓
IMPACT CLASSIFICATION

It partitions evolution into:

┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ PROOF-PRESERVING CHANGE      │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ CONDITIONALLY PRESERVING     │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ PROOF-EXPANDING              │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ PROOF-REDUCING               │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ SEMANTICALLY MATERIAL        │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ CONSTITUTIONALLY DISCONTINUOUS│
└──────────────────────────────┘

This gives HORTUS a formal vocabulary for evolution without collapsing every modification into either “safe” or “unsafe.”


---

VI. 🛡️ PROOF MONOTONICITY

R7.73 introduces:

PROOF_MONOTONICITY

A proof evolution is monotonic only when the newer domain does not invalidate the established proof relation.

For a bounded evolution:

D₁ → D₂

proof continuity may be established where:

PROOF(D₁)
∧
PRESERVATION(ΔD)
∧
ASSUMPTIONS(D₂)
∧
DEPENDENCIES(D₂)
∧
SEMANTIC_EQUIVALENCE

justify:

CONTINUED_PROOF(D₂)

But:

ADDING MORE STATES IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY MONOTONIC.

For example:

D₁ = {A,B,C}
D₂ = {A,B,C,D}

does not establish:

PROOF(D₂)

merely because:

PROOF(D₁)

was valid.

The new element D requires proof inclusion or a valid closure argument.


---

VII. 🔐 PROOF-PRESERVING EXTENSION

A particularly important case is:

D₁ ⊂ D₂

where the new states are introduced under an already proven invariant.

R7.73 defines:

PROOF_PRESERVING_EXTENSION

as an extension where:

∀x ∈ D₂:
    CONSTITUTIONAL_VALIDITY(x)

remains justified by the existing proof method.

This may occur through:

CLOSURE
PARAMETRIC PROOF
INVARIANT PRESERVATION
STRUCTURAL EQUIVALENCE
COMPOSITIONAL CERTIFICATE

but not through similarity alone.


---

VIII. 🦆 FOOL — CONTINUITY FALLACY

FOOL attacks:

> “The domain changed only slightly, therefore the proof remains valid.”



Rejected.

It also attacks:

> “The new state satisfies the same broad characteristics.”



Rejected unless the relevant equivalence is proven.

FOOL asks:

WHAT EXACTLY CHANGED?

DID THE DOMAIN PREDICATE CHANGE?

DID THE SEMANTICS CHANGE?

DID AUTHORITY CHANGE?

DID A DEPENDENCY CHANGE?

DID AN ASSUMPTION CHANGE?

DID THE PROOF METHOD COVER THE NEW REGION?

DID THE CHANGE ALTER THE MEANING OF VALIDITY?

Therefore:

SMALL DELTA ≠ SMALL CONSTITUTIONAL EFFECT


