XV. ⏳ EPOCH TRANSITION

Certificate continuity is always temporal.

C₁ VALID(E₁)

does not imply:

C₁ VALID(E₂)

unless the transition has itself been validated.

Therefore:

EPOCH_TRANSITION_VALIDATION

must establish:

E₁ → E₂

and determine whether:

PROOF_CONTINUITY(E₁,E₂)

exists.


---

XVI. 🌳 REVALIDATION FRONTIER

R7.72 established the:

CERTIFICATE_IMPACT_FRONTIER

R7.73 adds:

PROOF_REVALIDATION_FRONTIER

When evolution occurs:

DOMAIN CHANGE
     ↓
DEPENDENCY GRAPH
     ↓
CERTIFICATES
     ↓
PROOF CLAIMS
     ↓
AFFECTED FUTURE STATES

HORTUS identifies the smallest evidence-supported region that must be revalidated.

This avoids two opposite failures:

UNDER-VALIDATION

and:

UNNECESSARY GLOBAL REVALIDATION

The frontier must remain evidence-bounded.


---

XVII. 🧮 PARTIAL CONTINUITY

Not every evolution is globally continuous or globally discontinuous.

Therefore:

D₁
├── REGION A → CONTINUOUS
├── REGION B → CONDITIONAL
├── REGION C → REVALIDATION
└── REGION D → INVALID

is permissible.

This produces:

PARTITIONED_PROOF_CONTINUITY

The proof itself may therefore survive only over a formally preserved subdomain.


---

XVIII. 🔮 PROOF DOMAIN SHRINKAGE

A particularly safe evolution is:

D₂ ⊂ D₁

where states are removed from the proven domain.

If the original proof remains valid for every state retained:

PROOF(D₁)
∧
D₂ ⊆ D₁

then:

PROOF(D₂)

may be preserved, subject to assumptions and dependencies remaining valid.

This is a form of:

PROOF_RESTRICTIVE_MONOTONICITY

But even here:

SEMANTIC_CHANGE
DEPENDENCY_REVOCATION
ASSUMPTION_FAILURE

can invalidate continuity.


---

XIX. 🧬 PROOF DOMAIN EXPANSION

Expansion is stricter.

D₁ ⊂ D₂

requires evidence that:

D₂ \ D₁

is covered by the proof method.

Therefore:

OLD PROOF
+
NEW STATES

is insufficient.

Instead:

OLD PROOF
+
VALID EXTENSION ARGUMENT
=
POSSIBLE CONTINUITY

Otherwise:

REVALIDATION_REQUIRED


---

XX. 🛑 CONSTITUTIONAL DISCONTINUITY

R7.73 defines:

CONSTITUTIONAL_DISCONTINUITY

as:

> an evolution in which the assumptions, semantics, authority boundaries, invariants, proof method, jurisdiction, dependencies, or other constitutionally material properties change such that the prior proof can no longer legitimately establish the same claim for the successor domain.



Examples:

PROOF SEMANTICS CHANGED
AUTHORITY MODEL CHANGED
INVARIANT CHANGED
PROOF PREDICATE CHANGED
CRITICAL DEPENDENCY REVOKED
JURISDICTION CHANGED
ASSUMPTION BECAME FALSE
PROOF METHOD NO LONGER APPLIES

Then:

C₁ ✗ CONTINUITY

and:

REVALIDATION REQUIRED


