X. 🔐 DERIVATIVE AUTHORITY

R7.77 introduces:

DERIVATIVE_AUTHORITY

Authority inherited through composition is valid only within its explicitly proven derivation.

For example:

A → AUTH_A
B → AUTH_B

does not automatically produce:

S → AUTH_A + AUTH_B

Instead:

AUTH(S)

requires an explicit constitutional basis.

Possible result:

AUTH(S) = INTERSECTION(AUTH_A, AUTH_B)

or:

AUTH(S) = DELEGATED_AUTHORITY

or:

AUTH(S) = NONE

This prevents:

AUTHORITY ADDITION BY COMPOSITION


---

XI. 🧩 AUTHORITY CONSERVATION

R7.77 introduces a foundational principle:

AUTHORITY_CONSERVATION

A composition SHALL NOT create more authority than its constitutional inputs and explicitly established delegation permit.

Conceptually:

AUTH(S)
⊆
AUTH(A) ∪ AUTH(B) ∪ EXPLICIT_DELEGATION

And never merely:

AUTH(S) = AUTH(A) + AUTH(B)

because combining authorities does not automatically multiply their scope.


---

