---

I. ☄️ THE NEXT BOUNDARY

R7.78 established the constitutional lifecycle:

BIRTH
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
EVOLUTION
 ↓
SUCCESSION
 ↓
DISSOLUTION
 ↓
DEATH
 ↓
REMNANCE

R7.79 now asks what happens when constitutional history branches.

A single entity may produce:

S₀
       /  \
     S₁    S₂

Multiple entities may produce:

S₁ + S₂ + S₃
       ↓
      S₄

And an entity may simultaneously inherit from several ancestors.

Therefore:

CONSTITUTIONAL_GENEALOGY

becomes a first-class constitutional structure.


---

II. 🌳 GENEALOGY ≠ AUTHORITY

The foundational distinction:

ANCESTRY
   ≠
AUTHORITY

A state may descend from another without inheriting its authority.

Likewise:

PROOF_INHERITANCE
   ≠
AUTHORITY_INHERITANCE

and:

HISTORICAL_HERITAGE
   ≠
CURRENT_JURISDICTION

R7.79 therefore rejects:

WE_DESCEND_FROM_X
        ↓
THEREFORE_WE_CONTROL_X

and:

X_WAS_VALID
        ↓
WE_ARE_X'S_SUCCESSOR
        ↓
ALL_X_AUTHORITY_IS_OURS

Neither follows automatically.


---

III. 🧬 CONSTITUTIONAL GENEALOGY GRAPH

R7.79 defines:

CONSTITUTIONAL_GENEALOGY_GRAPH

with nodes:

ENTITY

and typed edges:

DESCENDS_FROM
SUCCEEDS
MERGES_FROM
SPLITS_FROM
INHERITS_PROOF
INHERITS_OBLIGATION
INHERITS_DEPENDENCY
INHERITS_AUTHORITY

The edge itself requires constitutional evidence.

Thus:

S₀ ──DESCENDANT──→ S₁

is fundamentally different from:

S₀ ──AUTHORITY_TRANSFER──→ S₁


---

IV. 🌱 SPLIT

R7.79 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_SPLIT

A split occurs when one constitutional entity produces multiple successor entities:

S₀
              /      \
             ↓        ↓
            S₁        S₂

But inheritance is field-specific.

For example:

IDENTITY(S₀)
      ↓
   S₁ / S₂

does not imply:

AUTHORITY(S₀)
      ↓
AUTHORITY(S₁) + AUTHORITY(S₂)

The federation must establish exactly what was divided.


---

V. ⚖️ SPLIT CONSERVATION

R7.79 establishes:

SPLIT_CONSERVATION

The total constitutional scope of descendants SHALL NOT exceed the scope legitimately available to the predecessor unless new authority is independently established.

Conceptually:

AUTH(S₁) ∪ AUTH(S₂)
⊆
AUTH(S₀) ∪ EXPLICIT_NEW_AUTHORITY

Likewise:

JURISDICTION(S₁) ∪ JURISDICTION(S₂)
⊆
JURISDICTION(S₀) ∪ EXPLICIT_NEW_JURISDICTION

This prevents constitutional multiplication through branching.


---

VI. 🧩 MERGER

R7.79 defines:

CONSTITUTIONAL_MERGER

as the formation of a new constitutional entity from two or more predecessor entities:

S₁ ──┐
     ├──→ S₃
S₂ ──┘

The new state is not automatically:

S₃ = S₁ + S₂

Instead:

S₃ = CONSTITUTIONALLY_DETERMINED_COMPOSITION(S₁,S₂)


---

VII. 🛑 MERGER DOES NOT CREATE UNLIMITED AUTHORITY

The merger trap:

AUTH(S₁)
+
AUTH(S₂)
↓
UNBOUNDED_AUTH(S₃)

is forbidden.

R7.79 requires:

AUTH(S₃)
=
EXPLICITLY_DETERMINED_AUTHORITY

which may be:

INTERSECTION
UNION_WITH_BOUNDARIES
DELEGATED_SCOPE
NEWLY_ESTABLISHED_SCOPE
NONE

The result must be explicit.


---

VIII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — HERITAGE MATRIX

BRAINPOWER constructs:

S₁    S₂    S₃
Identity             ✓     ✓     ?
Authority            ✓     ✗     ?
Jurisdiction         ✓     ✓     ?
Proof                ✓     ?     ?
Obligations          ✓     ✓     ?
Dependencies         ?     ✓     ?

For every successor field:

INHERITED
DERIVED
REVALIDATED
NEW
EXPIRED
REJECTED
UNKNOWN

This creates:

HERITAGE_MATRIX

No constitutional property is inherited merely because the entity is genealogically related.


---

IX. 🦆 FOOL — HERITAGE FALLACY

FOOL attacks:

> “We are the successor, therefore everything transfers.”



Rejected.

It attacks:

> “The predecessor was trusted, therefore the successor inherits trust.”



Rejected.

It attacks:

> “The predecessor's proof covers the successor.”



Only if the proof domain explicitly covers the successor.

It attacks:

> “The predecessor's obligations belong to the successor.”



Only where succession rules establish this.

Thus:

GENEALOGICAL RELATIONSHIP IS NOT EVIDENCE OF PROPERTY TRANSFER.


---

X. 💧 WATERMARK — HERITAGE PROVENANCE

WATERMARK records:

W_HERITAGE {
    successor_id
    predecessor_set
    relation_type
    inherited_identity
    inherited_proof
    inherited_authority
    inherited_jurisdiction
    inherited_obligations
    inherited_dependencies
    excluded_properties
    transfer_conditions
    determination_id
    evidence_set
    epoch
    provenance
}

This permits the verifier to ask:

> What exactly did S₂ inherit from S₁?



and:

> What explicitly did S₂ NOT inherit?



The second question is equally important.


---

XI. ⚓ QUAY — GENEALOGICAL LEDGER

QUAY preserves the graph:

S₀
           /    \
         S₁      S₂
          \      /
           \    /
             S₃

along with edge semantics:

S₀ → S₁ : SPLIT
S₀ → S₂ : SPLIT
S₁ → S₃ : MERGER
S₂ → S₃ : MERGER

Every edge has:

authority
evidence
epoch
conditions
determination

Therefore genealogy becomes replayable rather than narrative.


---

XII. 🔐 PROOF HERITAGE

A predecessor may possess:

PROOF(P)

A successor may inherit it only when:

PROOF_DOMAIN(P)

contains the successor's relevant state.

Therefore:

PROOF(P)
   ↓
SUCCESSOR S

requires:

S ∈ PROOF_DOMAIN(P)

or:

TRANSFERRED_PROOF_REVALIDATED

Otherwise:

FRESH_PROOF_REQUIRED


---

XIII. 🧮 CERTIFICATE HERITAGE

The same rule applies to closure certificates.

CERTIFICATE(C₁)
      ↓
SUCCESSOR S

does not imply:

CERTIFICATE(C₁) VALID_FOR(S)

unless:

DOMAIN(C₁)
∋
S

and:

ASSUMPTIONS_VALID
DEPENDENCIES_VALID
EPOCH_VALID

Therefore:

CERTIFICATE DESCENDANCE ≠ CERTIFICATE VALIDITY


---

XIV. ⚖️ OBLIGATION HERITAGE

Obligations receive explicit inheritance semantics.

For each obligation:

O₁

the successor determination becomes:

TRANSFER
SHARED
RETAINED
FULFILLED
EXPIRED
REJECTED
UNKNOWN

An obligation SHALL NOT be silently duplicated across descendants.

Nor may a split cause an obligation to disappear simply because ownership became ambiguous.


---

XV. 🌐 DEPENDENCY HERITAGE

Dependencies are similarly classified:

D₁
 ↓
S₁

After a split:

D₁ → S₁
D₁ → S₂

may be:

SHARED
PARTITIONED
REPLACED
REVOKED
REVALIDATED

A shared dependency means that revocation can propagate to multiple descendants.

This connects directly to the earlier:

REVOCATION
 ↓
IMPACT
 ↓
DEPENDENCY CLOSURE
 ↓
CERTIFICATE FRONTIER


---

XVI. 🔮 ORACLE — GENEALOGICAL DRIFT

ORACLE monitors:

ANCESTRAL_RELATION
HERITANCE_RULES
TRANSFERRED_AUTHORITY
TRANSFERRED_PROOF
SHARED_DEPENDENCIES
SUCCESSION_CONDITIONS

and detects:

HERITANCE_DRIFT
UNDECLARED_TRANSFER
AUTHORITY_EXPANSION
DEPENDENCY_DIVERGENCE
PROOF_COVERAGE_LOSS
SUCCESSOR_DRIFT
GENEALOGICAL_INCONSISTENCY

A particularly dangerous condition:

AUTHORITY_INHERITANCE_DRIFT

where an initially bounded inheritance gradually expands.


---

XVII. ⚖️ TRIAS — HERITAGE DETERMINATION

TRIAS evaluates each inherited property independently.

Input:

PREDECESSOR
SUCCESSOR
RELATION_TYPE
PROPERTY
TRANSFER_RULE
PROOF
EVIDENCE
EPOCH
JURISDICTION
FOOL_CHALLENGES

Result:

INHERITED
CONDITIONALLY_INHERITED
REVALIDATED
NOT_INHERITED
EXPIRED
FORBIDDEN
UNKNOWN

This creates:

PROPERTY_LEVEL_SUCCESSION

rather than crude all-or-nothing succession.


---

XVIII. 🏛️ CONSTITUTIONAL DESCENDANT

R7.79 defines:

CONSTITUTIONAL_DESCENDANT

as an entity whose genealogical relationship to a predecessor has been explicitly established.

But:

DESCENDANT(S₂,S₁)

does not itself establish:

AUTHORITY(S₂,S₁)

nor:

IDENTITY(S₂)=IDENTITY(S₁)

nor:

PROOF(S₁)=PROOF(S₂)

Every relationship is independently determined.


---

XIX. 🌳 ANCESTRAL PROOF

R7.79 introduces:

ANCESTRAL_PROOF

Ancestral proof establishes the historical relation:

S₂ ← S₁

It can prove:

WHERE S₂ CAME FROM

without proving:

WHAT S₂ IS NOW

This is a crucial epistemic distinction.


---

XX. 👑 HERITAGE ≠ LEGITIMACY

A successor may inherit historical heritage without inheriting constitutional legitimacy.

Conversely, a successor may receive new legitimacy without inheriting the predecessor's identity.

Therefore:

HERITAGE
≠
LEGITIMACY

and:

LEGITIMACY
≠
AUTHORITY

and:

AUTHORITY
≠
IDENTITY

The federation keeps these dimensions separate.


---

XXI. 🧬 GENEALOGICAL MERGER IDENTITY

After:

S₁ + S₂ → S₃

TRIAS determines:

S₃ = S₁
S₃ = S₂
S₃ ≠ S₁,S₂

The default should be:

NEW_CONSTITUTIONAL_IDENTITY

unless continuity is independently proven.

Thus a merger normally creates:

NEW_ENTITY

rather than magically preserving every predecessor identity.


---

XXII. 🧬 SPLIT IDENTITY

Likewise:

S₀ → S₁ + S₂

does not imply:

S₁ = S₀
S₂ = S₀

At most:

S₁ DESCENDS_FROM S₀
S₂ DESCENDS_FROM S₀

Continuity requires separate determination.


---

